{
  "_note": "SHAPE AND SNAPSHOT -- this file is the shipped kit manifest rendered for a factory to VENDOR. Copy it verbatim to `registry/kit.json` in your own repo at the moment you port kit files, and thereafter move it only when you deliberately port a new kit state. It is the record of WHICH kit you ported, and your own gate compares your tree against it.\\n\\nWhy vendor rather than read ours: a gate against this repo's live manifest makes your verdict a function of OUR working tree and our backlog, so our movement reddens your audit for a change you never took. A gate against your pin reddens it only when your tree diverges from your own declaration, which is the only thing you can act on.\\n\\n`classes` is the field you cannot port without: it tells you which files are `closure` (they must travel with the tool that imports them), which are `standalone` (a tool you run), and which are `seed` data (yours to fill in, never overwritten by an update). A pin without it leaves you unable to apply the port rule you were told to follow.\\n\\n`source_head` names the revision a pin was generated FROM, and it reads `unset` in this vehicle on purpose. A revision names a commit in the tree that generated the file, and this file was generated in the TEMPLATE repo, so writing that sha here would ship a value that resolves nowhere in your tree -- the one thing a vendored artifact must not do (the portability leg of `tests/test_template_sync.py` refuses it, and it is right to). The revision of the template repo whose kit you ported is not lost: it is the commit that published this file, and `git log -S<source_kit_version> -- registry/kit.json` in the template repo names it exactly. Your own pin's `source_head` is filled in only if you carry the generator.\\n\\nTWO DIGESTS, TWO QUESTIONS. `kit_version` is a digest over THIS pin's own (path, sha256, class) triples, so it describes the pin it sits in -- recompute it after you merge a fork, or it will name bytes your pin does not carry. `source_kit_version` is the digest of the template manifest this pin was ported FROM, and it is the value `git log -S` resolves in the template repo: it answers which kit state this came from, which a self-digest cannot. A delivered pin carries both. Before this split the transport wrote the source digest into `kit_version` beside a note asserting that value described the pin, and the two sentences contradicted each other two lines apart.\\n\\n`class_vocabulary` records the classes the pin's WRITER knew. It is not an inventory of the classes this pin uses: it is what lets a reader OLDER than the class say `this class was added after your reader -- port TEMPLATE/tools/kit_pin.py` instead of reading a real class as a misspelling.\\n\\nDo not hand-edit this file in your tree. It is generated by `tools/kit_manifest.py` in the template repo, and `tools/kit_pin.py` is the one reader -- a pin you typed by hand is a reference nobody can reproduce.",
